<h3>Commercial EV Fleet Charging</h3>
<p>Commercial EV charging is where the real money is. Fleet operators, retail properties, office complexes, and municipalities need multiple charging stations installed with load management systems, networking, and ongoing maintenance.</p>
<p><strong>Target customers:</strong><br />
- Delivery fleets (Amazon, UPS, FedEx contractors)<br />
- Ride-share vehicle fleets<br />
- Corporate campuses with employee charging<br />
- Retail properties attracting EV-driving customers<br />
- Municipalities electrifying public transit and service vehicles</p>
<p><strong>Marketing approach:</strong><br />
- Create a dedicated commercial EV charging service page with case studies<br />
- Attend fleet management and sustainability conferences<br />
- Partner with EV charging equipment manufacturers (ChargePoint, EVgo, Blink) as a certified installer<br />
- Develop load calculation and infrastructure assessment capabilities<br />
- Offer design-build services for commercial charging installations</p>
<h3>Local SEO for EV Charger Installation</h3>
<p>EV charger installation is a local service with local search behavior. Homeowners search "EV charger installer near me." Fleet managers search "commercial EV charging installation [city]."</p>
<p>Optimize your Google Business Profile by adding "EV Charging Station Installation" as a service. Create dedicated landing pages for each city you serve with EV-specific content. Get listed on EV charging directories and manufacturer installer locators.</p>
<h3>The EV Charger Sales Conversation: From Quote to Close</h3>
<p>EV charger consultations require more education than typical electrical jobs because most customers are first-time EV owners who do not understand their electrical needs. The sales conversation should address four questions:</p>
<p><strong>1. What level of charging do you need?</strong><br />
Level 1 (120V) charges 3-5 miles per hour — too slow for most EV owners. Level 2 (240V) charges 25-40 miles per hour and requires a dedicated circuit. Most residential customers need Level 2. Commercial customers may need Level 3 DC fast charging (150+ kW) for fleet operations.</p>
<p><strong>2. Can your panel handle the load?</strong><br />
A Level 2 charger draws 32-48 amps. Many older homes with 100-amp panels cannot accommodate this without an upgrade. The panel assessment is your upsell opportunity.</p>
<p><strong>3. What rebates and incentives are available?</strong><br />
Utility rebates range from $200-$1,000 for residential installations. The federal tax credit covers 30% of installation costs up to $1,000. Commercial installations may qualify for additional depreciation benefits. Maintain a current list of local incentives and help customers apply.</p>
<p><strong>4. What is the timeline?</strong><br />
Residential installations typically take 2-4 hours if no panel upgrade is needed. With a panel upgrade, the timeline extends to 1-2 days plus permitting. Set clear expectations upfront to prevent mid-project surprises.</p>
