<h3>Common Tracking Mistakes Electrical Contractors Make</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Tracking cost per lead instead of cost per booked job</strong>: A $50 lead that never answers the phone is more expensive than a $150 lead that books a $3,000 job.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Not tracking lifetime value</strong>: A customer who returns for annual maintenance is worth 5x their first job. Track the full relationship, not just the first transaction.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Attributing all calls to "direct"</strong>: When a customer sees your Google ad, visits your website, and calls the number on your truck two weeks later, that is a paid search lead — not direct traffic. Use call tracking numbers and CRM notes to capture the full attribution path.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Ignoring offline conversions</strong>: Commercial proposals won at in-person meetings often start with a website visit or LinkedIn connection. Ask every new commercial client: "How did you first hear about us?"</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Reviewing metrics monthly instead of weekly</strong>: Weekly reviews let you catch problems early. A Google Ads campaign with a broken landing page can waste $500 in a week. Monthly reviews let that waste compound to $2,000.</p>
</li>
</ol>
